Package: DrWrinch
Title: Fully Specified Bayes Factors for Process Tracing
Version: 0.0.2.0
Authors@R: c(
    person("Matias", "Lopez", role = c("aut", "cph"),
           email = "matiaslopez.uy@gmail.com",
           comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-0871-2841")),
    person("Jake", "Bowers", role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"),
           email = "jwbowers@illinois.edu",
           comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0002-4048-1166")),
    person("Daniel", "Gajardo Cooper", role = c("aut", "cph"),
           email = "drgajardo@uc.cl"))
Description: Computes fully specified Bayes factors for process tracing,
    as developed in Lopez, Bowers, and Gajardo Cooper (2026). One model
    underlies the package: each observation supports a working theory
    with probability theta, the share of the evidence that supports it,
    where the working theory claims theta above one half and a single
    rival claims theta at or below it. Because the rival claims a range
    of shares rather than one share, two Bayes factors follow, sharing a
    numerator and differing in how they treat that range: one averages
    the probability of the counts over the whole range, the other
    evaluates the rival at the single share that makes the counts most
    probable. The second is a lower bound and carries an error rate of at
    most one in twenty at a threshold of 20. Sensitivity analyses vary
    coding error, search bias, and the weight given the rival to show
    what a reader would have to grant for the conclusion to change.
    Named after Dorothy Maud Wrinch (1894--1976,
    <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dorothy_Maud_Wrinch>), whose joint
    papers with Harold Jeffreys (1919, 1921, 1923) developed the
    framework that became Jeffreys's theory of Bayes factors.
